Third-party code carried or derived here:

1. franka_vr/quest_bridge/oculus_reader/
   github.com/rail-berkeley/oculus_reader at commit
   1168953a0f4fe9ac761a0ef6139a511bb24c67a2, Apache License 2.0. Upstream's
   licence text is kept verbatim beside the sources as
   franka_vr/quest_bridge/oculus_reader/LICENSE.

   Modifications:
     - reader.py, install.py: imports made package-relative, because the
       package lives at franka_vr.quest_bridge.oculus_reader rather than at
       the import root.
     - reader.py, install.py: the APK comes from fetch_apk.fetch(), the
       user's data directory rather than the package, and is hash-checked
       before every install, the reader's own automatic one included.
     - reader.py: the Wi-Fi retry in get_network_device() calls
       get_network_device() as upstream main does. The pinned commit calls
       get_device(), which takes no arguments, so the retry raised TypeError
       instead of retrying.
     - fetch_apk.py is ours, added: see 2.
   Nothing else is changed. buttons_parser.py and FPS_counter.py are
   byte-identical to upstream.

2. The Quest APK (com.rail.oculus.teleop) is NOT distributed here.
   It is a build of upstream's app_source/ and it embeds Meta's Oculus Mobile
   SDK (lib/arm64-v8a/libvrapi.so, libvrinputstandard.so) under Meta's SDK
   licence, which is not Apache-2.0 and which we cannot restate or sublicense.
   franka_vr/quest_bridge/oculus_reader/fetch_apk.py downloads it from the
   upstream commit above and verifies sha256
   97b49f94682a732e14d131d50bcc7885e183d73b977e19a1752c28502519cd4b
   (4,865,715 bytes) before writing it. Upstream's code is Apache-2.0; the
   embedded libraries are Meta's, under Meta's terms, and installing the APK
   on a headset is an agreement with those terms, not with us.

3. franka_vr/quest_bridge/mapping.py began as a port of the front half of
   DROID's VRPolicy (github.com/droid-dataset/droid,
   droid/controllers/oculus_controller.py), which publishes no licence. It
   has since been reimplemented: no DROID function or block remains. It
   keeps DROID's names and ideas -- the signed `rmat_reorder` axis-map
   encoding and `vec_to_reorder_mat`, the
   forward-direction latch to inv(pose) with an identity fallback -- and a few
   one-line matrix expressions the maths forces.

